Fig. 6 
                          shows the relationship between the total strain amplitude ( Δε t /2 ) and the number of cycles to failure ( N f 
                         ) or fatigue life of different Mg alloys, including the present samples (Homo and ET150), extruded AZ31 [ 5 , 13 ], extruded NZ30K  [58] , rolled ME20  [7] , cast Mg (AZ80  [59]  and ZA105  [6] ) alloys. Besides, the TYS and elongation are also indicated in the figure to better understand the relationship between tensile properties and fatigue life. It is clear that the fatigue life increases with decreasing strain amplitude for various materials. Compared with Homo sample, ET150 sample shows a shorter fatigue life at a high strain amplitude of 0.8%, while it has a longer fatigue life at intermediate and low strain amplitudes (0.2%−0.4%). Besides, both the Homo and ET150 samples, as well as these reported Mg alloys do not fail up to >10 7  cycles at a strain amplitude of 0.1%, as marked by horizontal arrows. Especially, extruded NZ30K alloy shows a fatigue life of >10 7  cycles at a strain amplitude of 0.2%  [58] . Compared with other extruded Mg alloys (AZ31 [ 5 , 13 ] and NZ30K  [58] ), ET150 sample has a higher TYS but still shows a fatigue life similar to these extruded Mg alloys having lower strengths (AZ31 [ 5 , 13 ] and NZ30K  [58] ) at strain amplitudes of 0.3–0.6%. This is associated with the role of the good elongation of ET150 sample during cyclic deformation. Cast ZA105 alloy with a low elongation of 2.5% has a low fatigue life of ∼17 cycles at a strain amplitude of 0.8%, but it does not fail up to >10 7  cycles at a strain amplitude of 0.1%  [6] . Therefore, it could be inferred that low ductility of a material would worsen the fatigue resistance at high strain amplitudes but have little effect at low strain amplitudes in the strain-controlled fatigue tests.
